<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1331">1331</a>–<a href="/years/1371">1371</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Alexander was tsar of the Second Bulgarian Empire from 1331 to 1371. He made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, recovered lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and presided over a cultural flowering at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, including the illuminated Tetraevangelion of 1355–1356 and the Bulgarian translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasses-chronicle">Manasses Chronicle</a>. By dividing his realm between his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a> at Tarnovo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, he left it weakened in the face of the Ottoman advance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria" width="302" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asenovec1186 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_ALexander.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
